id: rz-ai-accelerator/drp-ai3-object-counter
category_id: rz-ai-accelerator
plugin: shell
user: root
_summary: Check if DRP-AI3 is working via the object-counter Q08 sample
_purpose:
    Check if DRP-AI3 is working by running the rzv-ai-applications
    object-counter (Q08) sample against a bundled real photo of a dog,
    using the upstream TEST input source (reads
    $SNAP_REAL_HOME/test.png via a gstreamer filesrc pipeline instead
    of a live USB/MIPI camera), so this can run headless/unattended.
    Success requires the sample to actually recognize the dog in the
    image (looks for "[INFO] dog detected." in its output), not just
    that the command ran without error.
    https://github.com/canonical/rzv_ai_applications
    .
    V2H and V2N share the same DRP-AI3 hardware/driver and the same
    object-counter command-line interface (<COCO|animal|vehicle>
    <USB|MIPI|TEST>); the only difference is the snap name
    (rzv-ai-applications on V2H vs rzv-ai-applications-v2n on V2N).
    This one job covers both generations - the caller must supply the
    installed snap's name via the DRP_AI_TEST_SNAP environ variable
    (e.g. in the launcher's [environment] section). V2L is not
    affected: it only ever has one possible snap
    (rzv-drp-ai-tvm-examples, see rz-ai-accelerator/inference), so it
    doesn't need this variable.
    .
    Note: TEST mode is a live-display demo mode that never exits on
    its own (it feeds the static image through an imagefreeze
    pipeline at 30fps in an infinite "Main Loop", waiting for a
    keypress to quit - see patches/apps/q08_test.patch upstream). This
    job runs it under `timeout` and inspects the captured output
    instead of relying on the process exit status. Confirmed on real
    V2H hardware (CID 202503-36406) that a 30s capture window reliably
    reaches "Main Loop Starts" with no [ERROR] lines.
    .
    Note: checkbox-ce-oem is itself a strict-confined snap and
    exports its own GST_PLUGIN_PATH/GST_PLUGIN_SYSTEM_PATH/
    GST_PLUGIN_SCANNER into every job's environment, pointing at its
    own bundled gstreamer runtime. That leaks into and hijacks the
    rzv-ai-applications subprocess spawned below, making GStreamer
    fail to find even core elements like "filesrc" (seen as a
    confusing "no element filesrc" error, unrelated to test.png or
    the actual rzv-ai-applications snap). Those vars are explicitly
    unset before invoking object-counter so it falls back to its own
    snap's gstreamer plugin path. Confirmed on real V2H hardware: the
    job fails with "no element filesrc" without this unset, and
    passes reliably with it.
    .
    Note: object-counter's own stdout is fully-buffered (not
    line-buffered) whenever it isn't connected to a real terminal -
    which is always the case for a checkbox shell job. That means a
    short message like "[INFO] dog detected.\n" (no explicit flush)
    can sit in libc's stdio buffer and never reach our captured output
    at all, even though detection actually happened internally.
    Confirmed on real V2H hardware: capturing via plain redirection
    never showed the detection line even after 60s, while allocating
    a pty for the child (forcing glibc's automatic line-buffering)
    showed it appear within ~2s every time. This job spawns
    object-counter through Python's pty module for exactly that
    reason - it is not optional.
imports:
    from com.canonical.plainbox import manifest
requires:
    manifest.has_rz_drp_ai3 == 'True'
    manifest.is_rz_series == 'True'
environ:
    PLAINBOX_PROVIDER_DATA
    DRP_AI_TEST_SNAP
command:
    set -o pipefail
    if [ -z "$DRP_AI_TEST_SNAP" ]; then
        echo "Fail: DRP_AI_TEST_SNAP has not been set! It must be the" \
             "installed object-counter snap name, e.g." \
             "rzv-ai-applications (V2H) or rzv-ai-applications-v2n (V2N)."
        exit 1
    fi
    cp "$PLAINBOX_PROVIDER_DATA/rz-ai-accelerator/test.png" "$SNAP_REAL_HOME/test.png"
    output_file="$(mktemp /var/tmp/renesas-rz-drp-ai3.XXXXXX)"
    trap 'rm -f "$output_file" "$SNAP_REAL_HOME/test.png"' EXIT
    env -u GST_PLUGIN_PATH -u GST_PLUGIN_SYSTEM_PATH -u GST_PLUGIN_SCANNER \
        python3 -c 'import os, pty, sys; snap = os.environ["DRP_AI_TEST_SNAP"]; argv = ["timeout", "-s", "INT", "30", snap + ".object-counter", "animal", "TEST"]; status = pty.spawn(argv); sys.exit(os.WEXITSTATUS(status) if os.WIFEXITED(status) else 1)' \
        > "$output_file" 2>&1
    rc=$?
    cat "$output_file"
    # TEST mode never exits by itself, so a timeout (124) or a
    # handled SIGINT (0/130) both count as a normal, expected end of
    # the capture window - only other exit codes are real failures.
    if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] && [ "$rc" -ne 124 ] && [ "$rc" -ne 130 ]; then
        echo "Fail: object-counter exited with unexpected status $rc"
        exit 1
    fi
    echo "Checking for errors ..."
    if grep -Eq "\[ERROR\]" "$output_file"; then
        echo "Fail: object-counter reported an error!"
        exit 1
    fi
    if ! grep -q "\[INFO\] dog detected\." "$output_file"; then
        echo "Fail: object-counter never recognized the dog in the test image!"
        exit 1
    fi
    echo "Success: DRP-AI3 object-counter correctly detected the dog!"
